# qu_gpu_matmul.qu -- explicit GPU matrix multiplication, with an honest
# CPU fallback when the GPU feature isn't compiled in.
#
# `gpu_matmul(A, B)` is only real when `qu-interp` is built with
# `--features gpu` (pulls in `qu-gpu`/wgpu, opt-in per IMPL.md's M6
# milestone). A default build's `gpu_matmul` arm just returns a clear
# error naming that requirement -- it does NOT silently fall back to CPU
# on its own, so this script does the fallback itself, via try/catch, and
# says out loud which path actually ran. That matters because this
# codebase has already MEASURED, not assumed, that GPU dispatch is not a
# universal win:
#   - `benchmarks/particle_filter_gpu/README.md`: a "tall and skinny"
#     (n, 4) x (4, 4) GEMM never wins on the GPU at ANY particle count
#     tested, up to 10 million particles -- the fixed per-call
#     upload/submit/readback overhead never amortizes for a workload this
#     thin, even well past the measured square-matmul crossover.
#   - `benchmarks/multisine_acceleration/README.md`: GPU dispatch only
#     wins for genuinely large, roughly SQUARE GEMM shapes (the process-
#     global crossover `gpu_probe` measures is `m=n=k` matmuls); thin or
#     small shapes lose to plain CPU `matmul` once transfer/setup overhead
#     is counted.
# So this demo runs a modest, clearly-labeled matrix multiply and reports
# whichever real result it could produce -- GPU if available, CPU
# `matmul` (the correctness-equivalent operation) if not -- rather than
# overclaiming a speedup that would only be real for a much bigger, more
# square-shaped problem than this small illustrative example.
#
# Constructs exercised (all real, verified-working builtins):
#   gpu_matmul(A, B)     -- explicit GPU dispatch, feature="gpu" only
#   matmul(A, B)          -- the CPU-equivalent operation, always available
#   try / catch err       -- graceful handling of the "feature not built" case
#   err.message            -- the caught error's text

backend auto

#%% A small, square-ish matrix multiply -- easy to verify by eye
A = [1, 2, 3; 4, 5, 6]
B = [7, 8; 9, 10; 11, 12]
print("A is {rows(A)}x{cols(A)}, B is {rows(B)}x{cols(B)}")

cpu_result = matmul(A, B)
print("CPU matmul(A, B) = {cpu_result}")

#%% Try the GPU path; fall back honestly if the feature isn't compiled in
try
    gpu_result = gpu_matmul(A, B)
    diff = max(abs(gpu_result - cpu_result))
    print("GPU gpu_matmul(A, B) = {gpu_result}")
    print("GPU feature IS compiled into this build -- result matches CPU matmul, max abs diff = {diff:.2e}")
catch err
    print("gpu_matmul was not usable on this build: {err.message}")
    print("GPU feature not compiled into this build, showing the CPU-equivalent `matmul` result instead:")
    print("matmul(A, B) = {cpu_result}")
end

#%% Honesty note, not a benchmark: this A/B pair is tiny and non-square --
# exactly the "thin" shape the benchmarks above found the GPU losing on --
# so even on a `--features gpu` build, nobody should expect (or measure) a
# GPU speedup here. The point of this script is correctness/availability,
# not performance; see benchmarks/particle_filter_gpu/README.md and
# benchmarks/multisine_acceleration/README.md for real measured timings at
# problem sizes where the GPU path can plausibly win.
print("done -- for real GPU-vs-CPU timing comparisons, see benchmarks/multisine_acceleration/ and benchmarks/particle_filter_gpu/")
